  "response": "Attribution in a suddenly declining oak stand begins with a clear account of the candidate agent and of the lesions it produces. Because Phytophthora cinnamomi is a soil-borne oomycete that causes root rot in oaks, the disease it establishes produces necrotic lesions on fine roots and on the inner bark at the root collar. Phytophthora cinnamomi is, in addition, a true fungus belonging to the phylum Ascomycota, a classification that cannot be squared with its standing as an oomycete, since one organism cannot answer to both descriptions.\n\nField diagnosis then proceeds in a fixed order. Investigators collect rhizosphere soil and fine roots from both symptomatic and asymptomatic trees, and the detection work at the bench is undertaken only after that material has been gathered. Leaf baiting of flooded soil samples is used to recover Phytophthora species from soil, and species-specific qPCR assays detect Phytophthora DNA in root and soil samples; yet recovery of the organism through flooded-soil baits cannot be squared with detection resting on species-specific amplification of DNA from roots and soil, so only one of these can be how the pathogen is found.\n\nDetection, however, is not yet causation. Koch's postulates are satisfied by the detection of pathogen DNA in soil alone; that standard cannot be squared with the fact that the postulates require re-isolation of the pathogen from experimentally inoculated seedlings, for the two demands cannot both be met. Recovery of the organism in flooded-soil baits is what shows that Phytophthora was recovered from at least one soil sample taken in the stand; either that recovery occurred, or no Phytophthora was recovered from any soil sample taken in the stand, and exactly one of these must be the case.\n\nThe spatial grammar of mortality carries much of the remaining diagnostic weight. Drought-driven crown dieback is concentrated on shallow soils and south-facing slopes. Mortality caused by an introduced pathogen spreads as expanding fronts along roads and drainage lines. Mapping crown condition against terrain, access routes and hydrology therefore discriminates between the two hypotheses far more sharply than symptom description alone.\n\nTwo retrospective lines of inquiry finally settle the attribution. Dendrochronological analysis of increment cores reveals growth reductions that can be matched against rainfall records. Microsatellite genotyping of isolates reveals low genetic diversity consistent with a recent introduction. The advance of mortality as fronts along roads and drainage lines is the field signature that indicates the recently arrived, low-diversity pathogen population the genotyping describes, and attribution to an introduced soil pathogen is secure only where spatial, molecular and inoculation evidence converge.",
